{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"secretary problem secretary problem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md","fileName":"Secretary Problem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Secretary Problem","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Search Algorithms]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Search Algorithms","Optimal Stopping Problem","algorithms.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"secretary problem secretary problem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md","fileName":"Secretary Problem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Secretary Problem","heading":"Secretary Problem","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The secretary problem is an [[Optimal Stopping Problem]] in which candidates arrive one at a time, must be accepted or rejected immediately, and cannot be revisited. The objective is to maximize the probability of choosing the best candidate.\n\nFor $n$ randomly ordered candidates, the strategy first observes and rejects roughly the initial $n/e$, using their best member as a benchmark. It then selects the first later candidate better than that benchmark. The method succeeds in about $1/e$, or 37 percent, of cases, and no competing rule has a higher probability under these assumptions.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Search Algorithms","Optimal Stopping Problem","algorithms.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Secretary Problem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__